1
00:00:09,320 --> 00:00:14,650
Let's jump into TCP's
Congestion Control.

2
00:00:14,900 --> 00:00:19,080
As a reminder, congestion in

3
00:00:19,080 --> 00:00:20,560
the network will occur and

4
00:00:20,560 --> 00:00:23,020
this leads to packets
being dropped.

5
00:00:23,020 --> 00:00:27,640
Our goal with congestion
control to say what if senders

6
00:00:27,640 --> 00:00:33,125
could detect this congestion
and back off as needed,

7
00:00:33,125 --> 00:00:39,080
and then we quickly introduced
TCP's approach to this,

8
00:00:39,080 --> 00:00:43,340
which is detect and adjust.

9
00:00:43,340 --> 00:00:46,120
Here senders can increase

10
00:00:46,120 --> 00:00:47,760
their sending rate until

11
00:00:47,760 --> 00:00:50,000
they detect that
congestion occurs,

12
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:54,000
then it goes about and
decreases the sending rate.

13
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:58,030
With TCP, the
feedback is implicit,

14
00:00:58,030 --> 00:01:00,450
and how they adjust

15
00:01:00,450 --> 00:01:05,120
is through something called
a window based approach,

16
00:01:05,120 --> 00:01:08,340
and we'll get into
each of these.

17
00:01:08,390 --> 00:01:11,060
Feedback. How do we

18
00:01:11,060 --> 00:01:13,920
know there's congestion
in the network?

19
00:01:13,920 --> 00:01:15,770
From a host standpoint,

20
00:01:15,770 --> 00:01:17,895
we don't really know

21
00:01:17,895 --> 00:01:22,380
the network is where the
congestion is happening,

22
00:01:22,380 --> 00:01:24,400
but we do have some signals

23
00:01:24,400 --> 00:01:26,590
that indicate that there
might be congestion.

24
00:01:26,590 --> 00:01:30,340
One of the main signals
is packet loss.

25
00:01:30,340 --> 00:01:33,520
Whenever we've seen
a timeout occurred,

26
00:01:33,520 --> 00:01:36,000
we've previously said we

27
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:38,025
are going to assume the
packet was dropped,

28
00:01:38,025 --> 00:01:40,135
and we have to retransmit it,

29
00:01:40,135 --> 00:01:43,765
and we've also seen
that with a duplicate

30
00:01:43,765 --> 00:01:48,100
ACK is also indicating
packet loss.

31
00:01:48,100 --> 00:01:50,010
If you can make the assumption

32
00:01:50,010 --> 00:01:53,740
that one of the main reason

33
00:01:53,740 --> 00:01:55,800
for packet loss is congestion,

34
00:01:55,800 --> 00:01:58,380
well then you can
correlate this to be

35
00:01:58,380 --> 00:02:01,480
a signal for congestion
in the network.

36
00:02:01,480 --> 00:02:04,280
Likewise, packet
delay, we talked

37
00:02:04,280 --> 00:02:07,600
about estimating the round
trip time and we get

38
00:02:07,600 --> 00:02:11,620
the round trip time
samples each time

39
00:02:11,620 --> 00:02:16,980
we send a packet and
get acknowledgment.

40
00:02:17,340 --> 00:02:21,060
If we see that
increasing over time,

41
00:02:21,060 --> 00:02:23,680
that is showing that
there's starting to be

42
00:02:23,680 --> 00:02:28,980
congestion that's happening
the speed of light,

43
00:02:28,980 --> 00:02:30,580
effectively the amount of

44
00:02:30,580 --> 00:02:33,435
time with no congestion to
get through the network.

45
00:02:33,435 --> 00:02:36,215
But then if you have congestion,

46
00:02:36,215 --> 00:02:38,000
as we showed last time,

47
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:41,800
you saw that buffer
increasing in size,

48
00:02:41,800 --> 00:02:44,160
you get into this queue,

49
00:02:44,160 --> 00:02:46,340
you're the last in the
queue as a packet,

50
00:02:46,340 --> 00:02:48,390
and it's going to take longer

51
00:02:48,390 --> 00:02:50,600
to actually get transmitted,

52
00:02:50,600 --> 00:02:54,265
and so we're seeing
longer and longer delays.

53
00:02:54,265 --> 00:02:57,000
Now, those are both
implicit signals,

54
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:00,840
but there is also the possibility
for an explicit signal.

55
00:03:00,840 --> 00:03:04,540
Explicit congestion
notification is something

56
00:03:04,540 --> 00:03:08,740
that if a router supports
it would be a possibility,

57
00:03:08,740 --> 00:03:10,860
but we'll make the assumption

58
00:03:10,860 --> 00:03:13,080
or most of the time we
make the assumption,

59
00:03:13,080 --> 00:03:16,695
that's just implicit signals.

60
00:03:16,695 --> 00:03:22,525
Now, given that when
we detect congestion,

61
00:03:22,525 --> 00:03:27,060
how do we know how much to send?

62
00:03:27,060 --> 00:03:30,520
We said this is a
window based approach.

63
00:03:30,520 --> 00:03:32,880
The host, the sender

64
00:03:32,880 --> 00:03:35,700
limits whatever amount
of traffic that

65
00:03:35,700 --> 00:03:38,100
it sends based on something

66
00:03:38,100 --> 00:03:41,140
called congestion
window or sea wind.

67
00:03:41,140 --> 00:03:44,280
You can look at this is
basically the last byte

68
00:03:44,280 --> 00:03:49,440
sent minus the last bite
that's been acknowledged.

69
00:03:49,440 --> 00:03:51,960
As the sender, you have to keep

70
00:03:51,960 --> 00:03:56,650
that less than or equal
to the congestion window,

71
00:03:56,650 --> 00:03:59,500
and we'll talk about how
this congestion window

72
00:03:59,500 --> 00:04:02,650
is calculated or adjusted.

73
00:04:02,650 --> 00:04:06,000
If you recall, we

74
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:07,300
heard the term window

75
00:04:07,300 --> 00:04:10,200
before when we talked
about flow control.

76
00:04:10,200 --> 00:04:13,400
Flow control is
something where we

77
00:04:13,400 --> 00:04:16,700
don't want to be
overwhelming the receiver.

78
00:04:16,700 --> 00:04:18,200
When the sender can send more

79
00:04:18,200 --> 00:04:20,160
than the receiver can receive,

80
00:04:20,160 --> 00:04:23,545
that is a different problem.

81
00:04:23,545 --> 00:04:28,375
TCP includes this explicit
mechanism, this field,

82
00:04:28,375 --> 00:04:30,780
the window size field inside of

83
00:04:30,780 --> 00:04:32,740
the TCP header for a

84
00:04:32,740 --> 00:04:36,220
receiver to be able to
signal to the sender;

85
00:04:36,220 --> 00:04:39,680
this is the amount of
bytes that you're allowed

86
00:04:39,680 --> 00:04:43,940
to have that I'm
capable of receiving.

87
00:04:43,980 --> 00:04:47,340
The difference is the
receive window is

88
00:04:47,340 --> 00:04:50,680
this explicit field
in the TTP header.

89
00:04:50,680 --> 00:04:52,740
The congestion window
is something that's

90
00:04:52,740 --> 00:04:55,740
just internal state on the host.

91
00:04:55,740 --> 00:04:59,310
Now, as a sender

92
00:04:59,310 --> 00:05:03,840
you have to be less than
both of these windows.

93
00:05:06,720 --> 00:05:11,180
Adjusting the
congestion window is

94
00:05:11,180 --> 00:05:13,220
effectively the approach in

95
00:05:13,220 --> 00:05:16,840
TCP is something called
additive increase,

96
00:05:16,840 --> 00:05:22,125
multiple multiplicative
decrease, or AIMD.

97
00:05:22,125 --> 00:05:26,290
The general concept here is,

98
00:05:26,290 --> 00:05:30,680
you basically keep increasing
the amount you send,

99
00:05:30,680 --> 00:05:32,720
you detect that
there's congestion,

100
00:05:32,720 --> 00:05:34,445
and then you decrease it.

101
00:05:34,445 --> 00:05:38,225
This particular approach to it,

102
00:05:38,225 --> 00:05:41,220
and it has been
shown to optimize

103
00:05:41,220 --> 00:05:44,485
congested flow
rates network wide,

104
00:05:44,485 --> 00:05:49,350
and it has this desirable
property of stability.

105
00:05:49,350 --> 00:05:52,200
The additive increase
means you're increasing

106
00:05:52,200 --> 00:05:55,905
the sending rate by one
maximum segment size,

107
00:05:55,905 --> 00:06:01,005
every round trip time
until loss is detected,

108
00:06:01,005 --> 00:06:03,360
a maximum segment size.

109
00:06:03,360 --> 00:06:07,005
Think of that as
the maximum packet,

110
00:06:07,005 --> 00:06:08,940
the length of the
packet that you can

111
00:06:08,940 --> 00:06:12,040
actually send into the network.

112
00:06:12,440 --> 00:06:17,490
The multiplicative
decrease is where you

113
00:06:17,490 --> 00:06:21,690
decrease the sending rate by
some multiplicative factor,

114
00:06:21,690 --> 00:06:24,000
so for example,
you're decreasing

115
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:26,700
it in half whenever
loss is detected.

116
00:06:26,700 --> 00:06:28,725
It's not just
decrease it by one,

117
00:06:28,725 --> 00:06:31,665
but you're decreasing
it by a larger factor,

118
00:06:31,665 --> 00:06:35,320
some factor of descent
rate, for example, a half.

119
00:06:36,230 --> 00:06:40,170
Illustrating the additive
increase shown here

120
00:06:40,170 --> 00:06:44,715
is our message diagram.

121
00:06:44,715 --> 00:06:51,660
The sender initially sets the
congestion window to one,

122
00:06:51,660 --> 00:06:56,200
so it can send one
packet into the network.

123
00:06:56,200 --> 00:07:01,835
When it successfully gets
a received acknowledgment,

124
00:07:01,835 --> 00:07:07,880
it now says, the network is
not as congested right now.

125
00:07:07,880 --> 00:07:10,835
It can increase, it
can try a little more.

126
00:07:10,835 --> 00:07:13,190
It increases the
congestion window to

127
00:07:13,190 --> 00:07:15,790
2, sends two packets.

128
00:07:15,790 --> 00:07:19,005
When it gets the acknowledgment
for each of those,

129
00:07:19,005 --> 00:07:21,360
it now increases the
congestion window

130
00:07:21,360 --> 00:07:23,505
and can send three packets.

131
00:07:23,505 --> 00:07:25,140
It gets those acknowledgments,

132
00:07:25,140 --> 00:07:28,660
and increases it to the four.

133
00:07:29,330 --> 00:07:36,540
This is a pretty discrete units,

134
00:07:36,540 --> 00:07:37,770
where you're sending

135
00:07:37,770 --> 00:07:42,000
these maximum size
packets every time.

136
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:45,000
But really you're going
to be sending packets of

137
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:48,525
different sizes and there's

138
00:07:48,525 --> 00:07:52,710
a different way that
you can calculate

139
00:07:52,710 --> 00:07:57,690
it based on the maximum
segment size and all that.

140
00:07:57,690 --> 00:08:01,680
It's not exactly one is
what you're increasing.

141
00:08:01,680 --> 00:08:04,300
That's additive increase.

142
00:08:04,300 --> 00:08:07,940
Multiplicative
decrease is whenever

143
00:08:07,940 --> 00:08:09,515
you detect the congestion,

144
00:08:09,515 --> 00:08:11,465
you decrease the send rate.

145
00:08:11,465 --> 00:08:14,795
What this does is over time,

146
00:08:14,795 --> 00:08:20,360
what you'll see is
this sawtooth pattern.

147
00:08:20,360 --> 00:08:23,645
You'll see the
multiplicative increase.

148
00:08:23,645 --> 00:08:25,640
You're sending, increase in

149
00:08:25,640 --> 00:08:30,025
ascend rate linearly
by one every time,

150
00:08:30,025 --> 00:08:34,545
where you're proving that
AI additive increase,

151
00:08:34,545 --> 00:08:36,120
and where you're proving for

152
00:08:36,120 --> 00:08:40,770
bandwidth started to hit
congestion and you drop it,

153
00:08:40,770 --> 00:08:42,420
so dropped it in half.

154
00:08:42,420 --> 00:08:45,210
Then you start probing
for bandwidth again.

155
00:08:45,210 --> 00:08:48,015
Maybe this time you get a
little further higher up.

156
00:08:48,015 --> 00:08:49,965
But then when you
hit congestion,

157
00:08:49,965 --> 00:08:52,230
you drop again and drop in half.

158
00:08:52,230 --> 00:08:55,290
It leads to this
saw tooth patterns.

159
00:08:55,290 --> 00:08:58,245
As we said, it's
important to reiterate,

160
00:08:58,245 --> 00:09:00,600
it's been shown to

161
00:09:00,600 --> 00:09:04,780
be this necessary
condition for stability.

162
00:09:05,120 --> 00:09:09,540
Now that makes
sense how it works,

163
00:09:09,540 --> 00:09:12,255
but why it works conceptually.

164
00:09:12,255 --> 00:09:16,290
If you recall this diagram
of this blue line,

165
00:09:16,290 --> 00:09:18,420
what we said was

166
00:09:18,420 --> 00:09:23,280
the efficient line and this
green line was the fair line.

167
00:09:23,280 --> 00:09:25,920
Anytime you get above

168
00:09:25,920 --> 00:09:30,270
this blue line is when you
start hitting congestion.

169
00:09:30,270 --> 00:09:32,295
No matter where we're starting

170
00:09:32,295 --> 00:09:33,780
in terms of Host 1 and Host

171
00:09:33,780 --> 00:09:37,515
2 sending, they'll
both increase.

172
00:09:37,515 --> 00:09:39,300
That increase is linear.

173
00:09:39,300 --> 00:09:42,780
They're all going to be
increasing at the same rate.

174
00:09:42,780 --> 00:09:45,000
Now when you do that decrease,

175
00:09:45,000 --> 00:09:48,240
when you start in
this congestion.

176
00:09:48,240 --> 00:09:51,150
Both Host 2 and Host
1 will see that,

177
00:09:51,150 --> 00:09:54,180
and the amount you're going
to adjust is going to

178
00:09:54,180 --> 00:09:58,110
be proportional to
their send rate.

179
00:09:58,110 --> 00:10:00,870
If Host 2 in this case

180
00:10:00,870 --> 00:10:05,250
is sending more over
here than Host 1,

181
00:10:05,250 --> 00:10:07,635
Host 2 is going to drop further.

182
00:10:07,635 --> 00:10:10,440
Half of its amount is going to

183
00:10:10,440 --> 00:10:14,115
be higher than half
of Host 1 's amount.

184
00:10:14,115 --> 00:10:17,910
That's why you see
this line like this.

185
00:10:17,910 --> 00:10:23,880
What will happen is you're
getting closer and closer,

186
00:10:23,880 --> 00:10:28,540
converging towards
this fair line.

187
00:10:28,760 --> 00:10:34,155
Now, TCP additive
increase that linear,

188
00:10:34,155 --> 00:10:36,765
it's pretty conservative,

189
00:10:36,765 --> 00:10:40,155
the amount you're probing
the network for bandwidth.

190
00:10:40,155 --> 00:10:45,945
It's slow to get
into congestion.

191
00:10:45,945 --> 00:10:51,570
What has been introduced is
something called slow start,

192
00:10:51,570 --> 00:10:53,940
it's a weird name,

193
00:10:53,940 --> 00:10:58,035
but increases the
congestion window

194
00:10:58,035 --> 00:11:02,655
every acknowledgment instead
of every round trip time.

195
00:11:02,655 --> 00:11:05,370
What this does is create

196
00:11:05,370 --> 00:11:12,855
this exponential increase
in up to some point.

197
00:11:12,855 --> 00:11:15,015
There is a threshold above which

198
00:11:15,015 --> 00:11:16,230
when you want to start getting a

199
00:11:16,230 --> 00:11:17,655
little more conservative,

200
00:11:17,655 --> 00:11:22,810
and start increasing
linearly again.

201
00:11:22,880 --> 00:11:27,880
When you hear TCP slow start,
this is what it means.

202
00:11:27,950 --> 00:11:33,580
With that, we conclude
the transport layer.

203
00:11:34,250 --> 00:11:39,910
Next time we'll get into the
application layer. Thanks.